I'd make the session's account part of every write's `WHERE` clause, using `req.user.accountId` from `auth.js:11`, and return 404 when no row matches. A 404 also avoids revealing whether another account's ID exists.

**`routes/orders.js:14`.** Orders already store `account_id`, so add it to the filter:

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

**`routes/accounts.js:6` and `:14`.** The only account a user may touch is their own. Reject any other `:id` up front, then write using the session value instead of the URL value:

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  if (req.params.id !== String(req.user.accountId)) return res.status(404).end();
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  if (req.params.id !== String(req.user.accountId)) return res.status(404).end();
  const row = await db.write("UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 RETURNING id",
    [req.user.accountId]);
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

The `String()` matters because `req.params.id` is always a string, while `pg` returns integer IDs as numbers.

One decision to make: this approach relies on every future query remembering the filter. All writes go through `db.write()`, so you could instead enforce scoping in Postgres with row-level security. `write()` would run `SET LOCAL app.account_id` after `BEGIN`, and policies would check it. That gives a database-level backstop, but it means changing the `write()` signature. I'd ship the per-query fix now and add RLS later if you want the backstop.

Enforce it in `sessionUser` (`auth.js:6`). Every request passes through that middleware, so joining `accounts` there and requiring `deleted_at IS NULL` locks out every user of a deleted account in one place:

```js
const rows = await db.query(
  "SELECT u.id, u.email, u.account_id FROM sessions s JOIN users u ON u.id = s.user_id JOIN accounts a ON a.id = u.account_id WHERE s.token = $1 AND s.expires_at > now() AND a.deleted_at IS NULL",
  [req.cookies && req.cookies.session]
);
```

The scoped route handlers from my last answer then need no changes, because a deleted account can never become `req.user.accountId`.

You still need to decide what happens to the account's open orders. Nothing cancels them now, so they stay live after deletion. `write()` runs one statement per transaction, so cancelling them atomically with the delete would mean extending `write()` to accept several statements.

I'd add one middleware in `app.js`, registered before `express.json()` and `sessionUser`. That way, malformed-JSON 400s and auth 401s get logged too. It writes one JSON line when the response closes, which is when the status, duration, and `req.user` are all known:

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  res.on("close", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      path: req.originalUrl,
      status: res.statusCode,
      ms: Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6,
      userId: req.user && req.user.id,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
      aborted: !res.writableFinished,
    }));
  });
  next();
});
```

I'm listening on `close` rather than `finish` so that requests the client abandons, or that hang as described in my last answer, still produce a line marked `aborted`. Don't log headers or bodies: the `session` cookie is a bearer token, and `billing_email` is personal data.

The open decision is where these logs go. If you already ship logs to a collector, match its format, or use its Express middleware (for example `pino-http`) instead of `console.log`. Which stack do you use?

